## Founding & History
Stoney Rise Wine Company is a family-run winery located in the Tamar Valley of Northern Tasmania, Australia. Joe and Lou Holyman purchased a vineyard in the Tamar Valley in 2004, which had been originally planted in 1986. Their goal was to grow and produce high-quality Pinot Noir and Chardonnay. The on-site winery was constructed in 2008. Joe Holyman's family has a history in Tasmanian grape growing and winemaking dating back to 1984. The vineyard they acquired was previously known as Rotherhythe Heights. The cellar door was recreated in 2020.

## People
Stoney Rise Wine Company is owned and operated by Joe and Lou Holyman. Joe Holyman is the principal winemaker. Lou Holyman manages the business and often greets visitors at the cellar door. Joe's family has been involved in Tasmanian winemaking since 1984. Joe has experience in various aspects of the wine industry, including working in restaurants, wine distribution, and as a buyer for The Wine Society. Lou's experience includes working hands-on in vineyards and in cellar door operations for South Australian wineries. Their son, Oscar, has also been involved, creating the "Yes Miss" Pinot Noir at age 16.

## Vineyard & Site
The Stoney Rise vineyard is located in the Tamar Valley, less than 1 kilometer from the Tamar River. The vineyard's elevation ranges from less than 10 meters at its lowest point to 30 meters above sea level at its highest. The rows are planted north/south with a spacing of 2.2 meters between rows and 1.2 meters between vines, resulting in a density of 4500 vines per hectare.

The total planted area is 7.35 hectares. This includes 0.75 hectares of Chardonnay, 5.7 hectares of Pinot Noir, 0.7 hectares of Trousseau, and 0.2 hectares of Grüner Veltliner. Other sources state slightly different total hectares, with one mentioning 4.2 hectares comprising 0.75 hectares of Chardonnay, 3.2 hectares of Pinot Noir, and 0.2 hectares of Grüner Veltliner. The vineyard was originally planted in 1986. More Pinot Noir, Grüner Veltliner, and Trousseau have been added in recent years.

The farming approach involves treating spur-pruned vines individually based on their crop load, aiming for an average of 1.5 bunches per shoot or 4.5 tonnes per hectare. Stoney Rise is conscious of its environment, using minimal sprays and having not used herbicides for close to ten years. They have also started using biodynamic preparations to improve soil health.

## Cellar Door Experience
The Stoney Rise Tasting Room is open Thursday through Monday, from 10 am to 5 pm. Bookings are recommended, though not always essential, especially for groups up to eight people. The cellar door, designed by Cumulus Studio, offers a relaxed and welcoming environment with modern architecture and views over the Tamar River (kanamaluka). Visitors can enjoy seated wine flights of their various wine ranges, as well as wine by the glass and bottle. The cellar door also features a "Drinks List" offering wines from other producers, including international selections, as well as non-alcoholic options and local beers.

## Food & Hospitality
Stoney Rise offers a curated snack menu from 12 noon to 3 pm. The menu includes items such as charcuterie, sardines, and cheeses. Notable dishes mentioned are a rich, creamy French onion dip, Wagyu bresaola with thin breadsticks, a three-cheese toastie slathered with onion jam, and sardines served with toast triangles. The focus is on high-quality ingredients, often paired with locally-baked sourdough. The tasting room has ample indoor seating, an outdoor fireplace, and an expansive lawn, providing a comfortable setting.

## Awards & Recognition
Stoney Rise wines have received positive reviews, with an average of 88.9 points in 65 community wine reviews on CellarTracker. The Real Review lists Stoney Rise with 9 Silver Ribbons and 15 Bronze Ribbons, along with 6 Top Ranks and 3 Top Values. Winemaker Joe Holyman was a finalist in the inaugural Young Gun of Wine Awards in 2007. Joe Holyman has earned a reputation as one of Tasmania's best wine producers.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Stoney Rise is conscious of its environment and employs minimal sprays, having not used herbicides for close to ten years. They have also begun to use biodynamic preparations on the soil to build soil and vine health. The cellar door building itself champions sustainability through the use of locally sourced and natural materials, and its thermal mass helps achieve natural heating and cooling. Their approach to winemaking is described as minimal intervention.

## Visiting Practical Info
The address for Stoney Rise Wine Company is 96 Hendersons Lane, Gravelly Beach, Tasmania 7276. The tasting room is approximately 25 minutes north of Launceston (via A7) and 60 minutes from the Spirit of Tasmania terminal in Devonport (via B71). The phone number for bookings and enquiries is +61 499 786 634. Their website is http://www.stoneyrise.com/. The cellar door is open Thursday to Monday, 10 am to 5 pm. Bookings are recommended for groups up to eight people. The vineyard participates in the Tasmanian Mixed Dozen freight scheme.

## Notable / Distinctive
Stoney Rise is noted for its family-run operation by Joe and Lou Holyman, with Joe being a fifth-generation Tasmanian with a family history in wine dating back to 1984. The winery is recognized for its dedication to vineyard health and a minimal input approach to winemaking. The cellar door, designed by Cumulus Studio, is described as architecturally stunning and more akin to a trendy wine bar, offering an elegant yet relaxed atmosphere with an outdoor fireplace and expansive lawn. A distinctive aspect is their "Drinks List" which includes a diverse selection of international wines, alongside their own, and a range of non-alcoholic options and local beers. The winery also produces a "No Clothes" natural wine range and the "Yes Miss" Pinot Noir made by their son Oscar when he was 16. Joe Holyman's winemaking philosophy aims to express the vintage and the "sense of place" of the vineyard. They have incorporated varietals like Trousseau and Savagnin from the Jura region of France into their offerings.

